WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.712
Imagine end-to-end tests you
never write. Or remember to run.

2
00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:06.949
Introducing Replay QA.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.056
Point it at your app.

4
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:13.460
No test suite needed. Just a quick chat.

5
00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:16.785
It explores your app in a real browser.

6
00:00:16.885 --> 00:00:20.119
Turns what it finds into
user journeys, and runs them.

7
00:00:21.008 --> 00:00:24.790
Then it runs on Replay's browsers.
On a schedule, while you sleep.

8
00:00:25.842 --> 00:00:28.477
Against staging, a
preview, even localhost.

9
00:00:29.258 --> 00:00:30.964
And on every pull request.

10
00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:35.594
Each run is recorded, then reviewed.
For what breaks, and what's slow.

11
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:38.787
Some bugs only exist in time.

12
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:42.380
Replay QA caught one in its own app.

13
00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.291
One update. Sixty-seven
milliseconds. Four dropped frames.

14
00:00:46.841 --> 00:00:50.334
No click. A background refresh
re-rendered the project list.

15
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.143
Twenty-five of twenty-seven
renders changed nothing on screen.

16
00:00:54.293 --> 00:00:57.960
A screen recording can't see
that. A time-travel recording can.

17
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:03.118
Another bug: one list, fetched two hundred
fifty-eight times in under three minutes.

18
00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:06.410
It never saved its place,
so it kept starting over.

19
00:01:07.383 --> 00:01:12.433
Every run, a recording. Every bug, a
root cause. Every fix, rerun to confirm.

20
00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:19.541
Replay QA. End-to-end testing, fully
managed. Catch bugs that only exist in time.
